\section*{O12 — Finding a quadratic nonresidue}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 003; 2026-09-30), the asserted result is that zeta, Dirichlet $L$-functions, and finite-order Hecke $L$-functions for $\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-3})$ have no zeros with real part greater than $7/8$, with poles excluded; In manuscript 2 (family 142; 2026-10-04), the asserted result is that dense polynomials over binary-specified prime fields have uniform deterministic complete factorization in bit time polynomial in degree and $\log p$, using the cited Hecke zero-free input.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 2.}
For any binary-encoded prime $p$ and nonzero densely encoded $f\in\mathbb F_p[x]$ of degree $n$, a uniform deterministic algorithm is claimed to output
\[
 f=c\prod_i g_i^{e_i}
\]
with $c$ the leading coefficient, distinct monic irreducible $g_i$ and positive integer multiplicities, in $O(((n+1)\lceil\log_2p\rceil)^{10^{12}})$ bit operations. Constants have an empty factor list. The computation is unconditional and deterministic; it receives neither an integer-factorization service nor a primitive-root oracle.

The separate algebraic reduction sets $L=\lceil\log_2p\rceil$, $B=20+(n+1)(L+1)$. If $p>B^{200000}$ and $n\ge2$, supply for every prime $q\le n$ a prime $\ell_q\notin\{2,3,p,q\}$ with
\[
 \ell_q\equiv1\pmod{12q},\qquad p^{(\ell_q-1)/q}\not\equiv1\pmod{\ell_q}.
\]
That reduction has bit cost $O((B+E)^C)$ for absolute $C$, where $E=\max(2,\max_q\ell_q)$, and $E=2$ when no such primes are required. This bound depends on the numerical size of the auxiliary primes; the full theorem additionally constructs sufficiently small primes.

\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
For every finite-order Hecke character of $F=\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-3})$, every Dirichlet character and the zeta function, the principal claim is
\[
 \operatorname{Re}s>7/8\quad\Longrightarrow\quad L(s)\ne0,
\]
where a principal character's pole at $s=1$ is allowed. The weaker displayed $11/12$ theorem follows within this scope. The cited arithmetic consequence gives absolute $A,C>0$ such that every odd prime $p$ has least positive quadratic nonresidue
\[
 n(p)\le C(\log p)^A.
\]
Thus $n(p)\ll_{\delta} p^{\delta}$ for each fixed $\delta>0$. Given binary $p$ and $a\in\mathbb F_p$, a deterministic algorithm of time polynomial in $\log p$ is further claimed to return a square root of $a$ or correctly declare it nonexistent. The half-plane assertion leaves zeros between the critical line and $7/8$ untreated.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 003; 2026-09-30), smoothed character sums are estimated in two ways: directly by large-sieve and recursive moment bounds, and through a Mellin transform involving the reciprocal of an $L$-function. Poisson summation and sextic residue characters align the two expressions so that their savings force continuation of the reciprocal.

For manuscript 2 (family 142; 2026-10-04), primary generators and norm equations reduce splitting to an odd-degree separator. Lifted ramification classes are filtered to keep extension degrees polynomial, and divisors at infinity distinguish roots; the analytic input bounds the auxiliary primes required by the finite algebraic algorithm.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The least-nonresidue bound yields deterministic polynomial time in the binary length of the prime: enumerate the stated polylogarithmic range and compute Legendre symbols until finding a nonresidue. The accompanying polylogarithmic upper bound on the least nonresidue is an additional quantitative conclusion.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 003; 2026-09-30), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: zeta, Dirichlet $L$-functions, and finite-order Hecke $L$-functions for $\mathbb Q(\sqrt{-3})$ have no zeros with real part greater than $7/8$, with poles excluded.

The central assertion is within the documented formal scope; the additional applications retain their own scope limits.

No Lean formalization is listed at this snapshot for manuscript 2 (family 142; 2026-10-04).

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{DirichletSevenEighths.lean}, \texttt{HeckeSevenEighths.lean}, \texttt{QuasiRiemannHypothesis.lean}, \texttt{SiegelZeros.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.DirichletCharacter.LFunction\_ne\_zero\_of\_seven\_eighths\_lt\_re}, \texttt{OAI.SevenEighths.HeckeFamily.LFunction\_ne\_zero\_of\_seven\_eighths\_lt\_re}, \texttt{OAI.SiegelZeros.WeightedTorusJets.dirichletRealZeroBound\_proof}, \texttt{OAI.SiegelZeros.WeightedTorusJets.exists\_absolute\_real\_zero\_gap}, \texttt{OAI.riemannZeta\_ne\_zero\_of\_seven\_eighths\_lt\_re}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

